Q = [
    # Group 10: Observability
    ("B60_10_1", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Observability"], "What is a 'Correlation ID' (Trace ID) and why is it critical for debugging a modern Full Stack application?", "In a distributed system, a single button click on the frontend might trigger an API Gateway, which calls an Auth Service, which calls a Payment Service, which queries a DB. If the Payment service throws a 500 error, looking at the raw server logs is useless because 10,000 other requests are printing logs simultaneously. A Correlation ID is a unique UUID generated by the API Gateway (or the Frontend) at the very beginning of the request. This UUID is passed via HTTP headers (`x-correlation-id`) to every single microservice. In your logging tool (Datadog/ELK), you search that single UUID and instantly see the exact linear path of logs across all 5 services for that specific user's click.", ["A unique UUID generated at the start of a request (Frontend or Gateway)", "Passed via HTTP headers to every downstream microservice and included in every log line", "Allows engineers to trace the exact execution path and pinpoint errors across a chaotic distributed system"], ["It is an ID that proves correlation equals causation"]),
    ("B60_10_2", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Observability"], "Users are complaining that the 'Checkout' button randomly does nothing. The backend logs show zero errors. The database shows zero failed transactions. The frontend uses a try/catch block around the API call, but no error is surfaced to the UI. How do you instrument the frontend to diagnose this 'Silent Failure'?", "Silent frontend failures are impossible to debug with backend tools. The issue is likely a JavaScript runtime exception *before* the API call is even made (e.g., trying to access `cart.items[0]` when `cart` is null, crashing the JS execution thread). You must implement a Client-Side Error Tracking tool (like Sentry). You capture unhandled Promise rejections and global `window.onerror` events. When the JS crashes on the user's browser, Sentry captures the full Stack Trace, the user's OS/Browser version, and a 'Breadcrumb Trail' (the last 5 buttons the user clicked) and sends it to your dashboard, allowing you to reproduce the exact frontend crash.", ["Backend logs are useless for JavaScript runtime crashes happening entirely in the user's browser", "The JS execution thread crashed before the `fetch()` was called, silently halting the UI", "Fix by implementing Client-Side Error Tracking (Sentry) to capture stack traces, breadcrumbs, and unhandled rejections"], ["You just add `console.log` and ask the users to open developer tools"]),
    ("B60_10_3", "implement", "medium", "implement", ["Observability"], "How do you implement 'Distributed Tracing' using OpenTelemetry in a Node.js / React application?", "You install the OpenTelemetry SDK on both the frontend and backend. The React app is configured to automatically wrap every `fetch` request, generating a W3C `traceparent` HTTP header. The Node.js backend receives this header, extracts the Trace ID, and automatically creates 'Spans' (timing blocks) for every database query or external API call it makes. Both ends asynchronously stream these spans to a collector (Jaeger/Datadog). This creates a visual Gantt chart showing exactly how many milliseconds were spent in the browser network queue, the Node.js event loop, and the PostgreSQL query.", ["Frontend SDK auto-injects W3C `traceparent` headers into outgoing fetch requests", "Backend SDK extracts the Trace ID and wraps DB queries/logic into timed 'Spans'", "Asynchronously streams spans to a collector (Jaeger/Datadog) to visualize end-to-end latency Gantt charts"], ["You use a compass and a map to trace the data packets"]),
    ("B60_10_4", "tradeoff", "hard", "tradeoff", ["Observability"], "What is the tradeoff of implementing 100% Request Logging (logging every HTTP request/response payload) in the API Gateway?", "Logging 100% of payloads makes debugging trivial; you can see exactly what JSON the user sent when an error occurred. The severe tradeoff is Data Privacy/Security and immense infrastructural cost. Users will send passwords, credit card numbers, and PII (Personally Identifiable Information) in POST requests. The API Gateway will blindly write this sensitive data into plaintext log files (Splunk/ELK), violating GDPR, HIPAA, and PCI compliance. You must implement aggressive Log Masking/Redaction (regex stripping PII) before writing to the log, which consumes massive CPU at scale.", ["100% payload logging provides perfect debugging visibility", "Tradeoff: Causes catastrophic PII/Compliance breaches (logging plaintext passwords and credit cards)", "Tradeoff: Requires heavy CPU overhead to run Regex Masking/Redaction on every single payload"], ["Logging 100% of requests makes the internet run out of ink"]),

    # Group 11: End-to-End Integration & Edge Cases
    ("B60_11_1", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["End-to-End Latency"], "You have a frontend dashboard displaying 'Events Created Today'. A user in Tokyo creates an event at 8:00 AM JST. A user in New York looks at the dashboard at 8:00 PM EST the previous day. How do you architect the end-to-end Timezone handling so both users see accurate data?", "You must enforce a strict separation of concerns regarding Timezones. The Backend Database must NEVER store local times; all timestamps must be stored exclusively in UTC (`2023-10-05T23:00:00Z`). The API must transmit dates as ISO-8601 UTC strings. The Frontend is solely responsible for localization. The browser reads the user's local timezone (via `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone`) and converts the UTC string to JST for the Tokyo user, and EST for the New York user at the exact moment of rendering.", ["Backend/Database strictly stores and processes all dates in UTC", "API transmits dates exclusively as ISO-8601 UTC strings", "Frontend strictly handles localization, converting UTC to the user's local browser timezone right before rendering"], ["You just store the timezone as a string like 'Tokyo Time' in the DB"]),
    ("B60_11_2", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["End-to-End Debugging"], "A user clicks 'Download PDF Report'. The frontend shows a loading spinner, makes a GET request, and receives a 200 OK. However, instead of downloading a file, the browser console prints a massive string of random characters, and the UI hangs. What integration mistake caused this?", "The frontend made an AJAX/Fetch request for a binary file (PDF) but did not instruct the browser how to handle the Blob, or the backend failed to set the correct `Content-Disposition` headers. By default, `fetch()` attempts to read the response as text/JSON. The massive random string is the binary PDF data parsed as UTF-8 text, which crashes the JS engine. To fix this, the Backend must set `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=report.pdf` and `Content-Type: application/pdf`. The Frontend should ideally just use a standard `<a href='/api/report'>` link, or if using `fetch`, must call `response.blob()` and dynamically create a hidden `<a>` tag with a `URL.createObjectURL(blob)` to force the browser download.", ["Backend failed to set `Content-Disposition: attachment` or Frontend mishandled the binary data via AJAX", "Fetch parses binary data as UTF-8 text, crashing the browser console", "Fix: Use `response.blob()` and `URL.createObjectURL()` to programmatically trigger the download, or just use a standard anchor link"], ["The PDF was written in a foreign language"]),
    ("B60_11_3", "implement", "medium", "implement", ["Asynchronous Workflows"], "How do you implement an end-to-end flow for an API request that takes 2 minutes to process (e.g., generating an AI video) without timing out the HTTP connection?", "You cannot hold an HTTP connection open for 2 minutes; browsers or load balancers will sever it with a 504 Timeout. You must implement the 'Async Polling' or 'Webhook' pattern. 1) Frontend POSTs the request. 2) Backend immediately returns `202 Accepted` with a `job_id`. 3) Backend queues the heavy task in a background worker (Celery/BullMQ). 4) Frontend enters a polling loop (e.g., `GET /jobs/{job_id}` every 3 seconds) or connects to a WebSocket. 5) When the worker finishes, it updates the DB status to 'Completed'. 6) The next frontend poll receives a 200 OK with the final Video URL.", ["HTTP connections cannot survive long-running (2-minute) compute tasks due to proxy/browser timeouts", "Backend must return HTTP 202 Accepted instantly with a `job_id` and offload compute to a background queue", "Frontend implements polling (or WebSockets) to check the `job_id` status until it completes"], ["Just increase the timeout setting in Google Chrome to 5 minutes"]),
    ("B60_11_4", "tradeoff", "hard", "tradeoff", ["System Architecture"], "What is the tradeoff of using Webhooks versus Client Polling for third-party integrations (e.g., waiting for Stripe to confirm a payment)?", "Client Polling (frontend asking the backend 'Is it done yet?' every 2 seconds) is incredibly easy to implement and bypasses complex NAT/firewall issues. The tradeoff is massive wasted compute/bandwidth on the backend handling thousands of empty 'Not Done Yet' requests. Webhooks reverse the flow: Stripe actively pushes an HTTP POST to your backend exactly when the event occurs. This is perfectly efficient (zero wasted requests). The severe tradeoff is Security and Resiliency. Your webhook endpoint must be public, requiring cryptographic signature validation to prevent spoofing. If your backend is down when Stripe fires the webhook, the event is lost unless Stripe implements robust retry queues.", ["Polling is easy and firewall-friendly, but wastes massive server compute on empty requests", "Webhooks are perfectly efficient (event-driven push)", "Tradeoff: Webhooks require exposing public endpoints, complex cryptographic validation, and robust retry logic for downtime"], ["Webhooks use spider silk, polling uses fishing poles"]),
    ("B60_11_5", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["End-to-End Latency"], "What is 'TCP Slow Start' and how does it impact frontend performance on new API connections?", "When a browser opens a new TCP connection to an API, the server does not immediately blast the full payload at maximum bandwidth. To avoid congesting unknown network routers, TCP starts by sending a very small amount of data (the congestion window), waits for an ACK from the client, and then doubles the window size iteratively until it hits the network's limit. This 'Slow Start' means that downloading a 500KB JSON file on a brand new connection will take significantly longer (requiring multiple round trips) than downloading it over a 'warmed-up', persistent Keep-Alive connection.", ["TCP intentionally throttles initial data transmission to probe network congestion limits", "The transmission window doubles on each successful ACK (exponential growth)", "Brand new connections suffer high latency for large payloads; mitigatable by HTTP Keep-Alive connection pooling"], ["It means the server has to warm up its engine like a car"]),
    ("B60_11_6", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["Frontend/Backend Integration"], "Your React app submits a JSON payload to a Go backend. The user types their name as 'O'Connor'. The backend database saves it as 'O&#39;Connor'. When the React app fetches and displays the data, it literally says 'O&#39;Connor' on the screen. Where did the encoding pipeline break?", "The backend applied HTML Entity Encoding (escaping quotes to `&#39;`) before saving to the database to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS). This is an archaic anti-pattern. React automatically escapes strings before rendering them to the DOM, making it natively immune to XSS. By escaping the data in the backend, the backend destroyed the raw data integrity. When React renders `&#39;`, it escapes the ampersand, rendering the literal HTML entity text. To fix this: the backend must ALWAYS store raw, unescaped data in the database, and rely on the frontend framework (React/Vue) to handle contextual output encoding at render time.", ["Backend incorrectly applied HTML escaping before saving to the DB (destroying data integrity)", "React automatically escapes raw data on render; double-escaping causes literal entity rendering", "Fix: Backend must store raw data; Frontend handles output encoding contextually at render time"], ["The database doesn't support Irish names"]),
    ("B60_11_7", "implement", "hard", "implement", ["System Architecture"], "How do you implement 'Soft Deletes' across a full stack system, and what is the massive risk to relational database performance?", "Instead of issuing a SQL `DELETE`, the backend sets a `deleted_at = NOW()` timestamp column. The frontend UI filters these out. To implement this globally, you apply global ORM scopes (`WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`) so developers don't accidentally fetch deleted records. The massive risk is Database Indexing. A table with 100 million rows might have 90 million 'deleted' rows. If your indices do not include the `deleted_at` column, every frontend query will perform a massive sequential scan over millions of deleted rows, tanking performance. You must implement Partial/Filtered Indices (`CREATE INDEX ON table (user_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`) to exclude them from the B-Tree.", ["Instead of `DELETE`, set a `deleted_at` timestamp and apply global ORM filters", "Allows data recovery and historical auditing", "Massive Risk: Unoptimized queries scan millions of deleted rows. Must use Partial/Filtered Indices to exclude deleted data from the B-Tree"], ["Soft deletes mean whispering to the database so it doesn't get scared"]),
    ("B60_11_8", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["System Architecture"], "What is the architectural tradeoff of storing User Session state in a centralized Redis cluster versus using stateless JWTs?", "Redis Session Storage guarantees immediate, absolute control. If an admin clicks 'Ban User', the session is deleted from Redis, and the user is locked out on their very next API click. The tradeoff is infrastructure dependency and latency: EVERY single API request across the entire system must perform a network hop to Redis to validate the session, creating a massive central bottleneck. Stateless JWTs eliminate this DB lookup completely—microservices verify the cryptographic signature in memory in 1 millisecond. The severe tradeoff of JWTs is that they cannot be instantly revoked without complex architecture (like token denylists).", ["Redis Sessions: Absolute, instant revocation and state control", "Tradeoff: Creates a central database bottleneck; every API call requires a network hop to Redis", "Stateless JWTs: Infinite scalability and zero DB latency, but extremely difficult to instantly revoke"], ["Redis stores sessions in red memory, JWTs store them in green memory"]),
    ("B60_11_9", "scenario", "hard", "scenario", ["Full Stack Performance"], "You build a dashboard for a logistics company. The frontend must fetch 50,000 shipment coordinates and plot them on a map. Fetching the JSON API crashes the browser tab because the payload is 50MB and parsing it freezes the main thread. How do you re-architect this?", "Sending 50MB of uncompressed JSON to a browser is an anti-pattern. You must re-architect both the network and rendering layers. 1) Backend: Compress the payload using GZIP/Brotli, reducing network transit from 50MB to ~5MB. 2) Data Format: Switch from verbose JSON (with repeated keys) to a flat binary format like Protocol Buffers, FlatBuffers, or even a raw CSV string. 3) Frontend Parsing: Do not parse the data on the UI thread. Use a 'Web Worker' to download and parse the binary data in the background, then pass only the rendering coordinates to the main thread (or use WebGL/Canvas to render it efficiently without DOM nodes).", ["Do not send massive JSON arrays; parsing them blocks the browser's main UI thread (freezing the tab)", "Backend: Compress via Brotli and use dense binary formats (Protobuf/Flatbuffers) or flat arrays", "Frontend: Use a Web Worker to parse the data off the main thread, and WebGL to render massive datasets"], ["You compress the shipments into a single box"]),
    ("B60_11_10", "explain", "medium", "explain", ["System Architecture"], "Explain the difference between a 'Monolith', a 'Modular Monolith', and 'Microservices'.", "A Monolith deploys all application code (Auth, Billing, Inventory) as a single process accessing a single database. It is easy to deploy but becomes a tangled 'Big Ball of Mud' as the team grows. Microservices split the app into physically isolated services running on different servers, communicating via HTTP/Kafka. This allows independent scaling and deployment, but introduces massive network complexity, distributed transactions, and DevOps overhead. A Modular Monolith is the middle ground: it deploys as a single physical process, but the codebase is strictly segregated into isolated modules (e.g., using Domain-Driven Design). Modules cannot access each other's databases directly; they must use internal programmatic APIs. It provides the clean boundaries of microservices without the network latency overhead.", ["Monolith: Single process, single DB (easy deploy, prone to tangled code)", "Microservices: Physically isolated services communicating via HTTP (independent scaling, massive network/devops complexity)", "Modular Monolith: Single process, but strictly isolated codebase domains (clean boundaries without network overhead)"], ["A monolith is made of stone, microservices are made of plastic"]),

    # Filler to reach exactly 100 questions (Need 18 more to hit 100 exactly)
    # Continuing Group 11
    ("B60_11_11", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Deployment & Architecture"], "What is the purpose of a Reverse Proxy (like Nginx) in front of a Node.js API?", "Node.js (Express) is terrible at serving static files, managing SSL/TLS certificates, and protecting itself from slow-loris DDoS attacks. A Reverse Proxy sits in front of the Node app. It handles the heavy cryptographic math for HTTPS, serves static assets (images/CSS) instantly from RAM, and buffers incoming HTTP requests, only passing complete, validated requests to the fragile Node.js backend. This drastically improves security and performance.", ["Handles SSL/TLS termination, offloading cryptographic math from the app server", "Serves static files (HTML/CSS/Images) highly efficiently", "Buffers slow client connections, protecting the backend from DDoS and resource exhaustion"], ["It proxies the internet in reverse so hackers get confused"]),
    ("B60_11_12", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["System Architecture"], "Your API uses Redis as a cache. If the database takes 2 seconds to execute a query, caching it in Redis reduces latency to 10ms. However, if the Database server physically crashes, your API goes completely offline, even for data that is perfectly cached in Redis. Why?", "This is a failure of 'Graceful Degradation' and hardcoded dependency ordering. The Backend code is likely establishing a strict DB connection pool on startup or explicitly validating the DB connection *before* checking the cache. If the DB is unreachable, the application throws a fatal exception before the cache logic even executes. To fix this, the cache layer must completely wrap the DB layer, and the application must be architected to serve stale cache data (or generic fallback data) even if the primary datastore connection fails entirely.", ["Hardcoded dependency logic: The app checks/requires the DB connection before executing cache lookups", "A DB crash throws a fatal exception, taking down the API", "Fix: Decouple the cache lookup from DB availability; serve stale cache gracefully if the DB is down"], ["Redis refuses to work if the database is asleep"]),
    ("B60_11_13", "implement", "hard", "implement", ["System Architecture"], "How do you implement secure 'Service-to-Service' authorization (e.g., the Billing microservice calling the Email microservice) without user intervention?", "You cannot use User JWTs, because background CRON jobs or async workers do not have an active user context. You must implement the 'OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials Flow' (Machine-to-Machine / M2M Auth). The Billing service is registered as an 'App' in the Identity Provider (Okta/Auth0) with its own Client ID and Client Secret. When Billing needs to email someone, it authenticates itself against Auth0 using its Secret, receives a short-lived M2M JWT, and passes that JWT to the Email service. The Email service verifies the signature and checks the scopes (e.g., `scope: send_email`) to authorize the machine request.", ["Cannot use User JWTs for asynchronous background tasks", "Implement OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials Flow (Machine-to-Machine Auth)", "Microservices authenticate themselves using Client Secrets to obtain scoped JWTs for internal API calls"], ["You just trust all internal servers because they are on the same WiFi"]),
    ("B60_11_14", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Data Flow"], "What is the tradeoff of using standard HTTP REST versus gRPC for internal microservice-to-microservice communication?", "HTTP REST uses JSON, which is human-readable, incredibly easy to debug with cURL/Postman, and universally supported. The tradeoff is performance: JSON is a massive, uncompressed string format that requires heavy CPU parsing, and REST lacks strict typing/contracts. gRPC uses Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) over HTTP/2. The data is serialized into dense binary, making it 5-10x faster to transmit and parse, and the schema contract is mathematically enforced. The severe tradeoff is debugging difficulty (you cannot read binary payloads in a terminal) and it requires compiling Protobuf stubs for every language in your stack.", ["REST (JSON): Human-readable, easy to debug, but verbose and slow to parse", "gRPC (Protobuf): Dense binary, extremely fast, enforces strict schema contracts", "Tradeoff: gRPC is unreadable without specific tooling and requires cross-language code generation overhead"], ["gRPC stands for general REST protocol"]),
    ("B60_11_15", "scenario", "hard", "scenario", ["Data Flow"], "A user changes their username on your frontend. The SPA updates the global UI state instantly. However, if they hit F5 (refresh), their username reverts to the old one for about 3 seconds, and then snaps back to the new one. What distributed systems issue is causing this UI flicker?", "This is an 'Eventual Consistency / Read Replica Replication Lag' issue. The frontend sent a POST request. The API routed the write to the Primary Database (Master). The Primary successfully updated and returned 200 OK. However, when the user hits F5, the GET request is routed to a Database Read Replica. It takes ~3 seconds for the Primary to replicate the new data to the Replica. The Read Replica returns the stale old username. To fix this, the backend must implement 'Read-After-Write Consistency' (e.g., forcing the user's reads to query the Primary DB for 5 seconds after a write, or caching the new value in Redis).", ["Replication Lag: The POST hit the Primary DB, but the subsequent GET hit a stale Read Replica", "The Replica hasn't synchronized the new data yet, serving old data to the UI", "Fix: Enforce Read-After-Write consistency by routing the user's immediate reads to the Primary DB or Redis cache"], ["The database was daydreaming"]),
    ("B60_11_16", "explain", "medium", "explain", ["System Architecture"], "Explain 'Database Connection Pooling' and why it is critical for a high-traffic API server.", "Opening a brand new TCP connection to a PostgreSQL database is an extremely expensive, slow operation involving authentication handshakes. If a Node.js server receives 1,000 concurrent API requests and attempts to open 1,000 new database connections simultaneously, the DB will run out of memory and crash instantly. A Connection Pool maintains a fixed number of persistent, already-open connections (e.g., 20). When an API request comes in, it 'borrows' an open connection, runs the fast SQL query, and instantly returns it to the pool. This shields the database from connection exhaustion and eliminates handshake latency.", ["Opening new DB connections is incredibly slow and CPU/memory intensive", "Without limits, traffic spikes will exhaust DB memory and crash the server", "Connection Pools maintain a small, fixed number of persistent connections, recycling them across thousands of rapid API requests"], ["It is a literal swimming pool of hard drives"]),
    ("B60_11_17", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Deployment & Architecture"], "What is the purpose of a 'Content Delivery Network' (CDN) in a Full Stack application?", "A CDN is a globally distributed network of edge servers. If your main application server is in New York, a user in Tokyo will experience 200ms of latency just for the data to travel across the ocean. A CDN stores copies of your static assets (Images, CSS, React JS bundles) in a datacenter physically located in Tokyo. When the Tokyo user requests the site, the CDN serves the files locally in 10ms, drastically improving load times and completely removing the static file bandwidth burden from your main New York server.", ["A global network of servers that caches static assets physically close to end users", "Drastically reduces network latency (speed of light over oceans) for users far from the origin server", "Offloads massive bandwidth and CPU costs from the primary backend server"], ["It is a network that delivers television content"]),
    ("B60_11_18", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["Application Security"], "Your team uses JWTs for authentication. A security researcher finds that if they take a valid JWT, change the algorithm header to `alg: none`, modify the user ID to `admin`, and send it to your API, the API accepts it as a valid Admin token. What critical flaw in the backend JWT validation caused this?", "This is the classic 'Algorithm Confusion / None Algorithm' vulnerability. The backend JWT library was configured to blindly trust the `alg` header provided by the client. The attacker stripped the cryptographic signature from the token and told the server 'This token doesn't use encryption (`none`)'. The poorly configured backend library saw `none`, skipped the signature verification process entirely, and parsed the fake payload. To fix this, backend JWT libraries MUST be hardcoded to only accept a specific secure algorithm (e.g., `HS256` or `RS256`) and explicitly reject `none`.", ["The backend blindly trusted the client-provided `alg` header in the token", "Attacker set `alg: none` and stripped the signature, bypassing cryptographic verification", "Fix: Hardcode the backend verifier to explicitly require and enforce a specific algorithm (e.g., HS256)"], ["The JWT expired and defaulted to admin"]),
    ("B60_11_19", "implement", "hard", "implement", ["System Architecture"], "How do you implement 'Long Polling' to simulate real-time updates when WebSockets are strictly blocked by a corporate firewall?", "If WebSockets (which use the `ws://` protocol) are blocked, you must use Long Polling over standard HTTP. 1) The frontend sends an HTTP GET request to `/api/updates`. 2) The Backend does NOT respond immediately. It holds the TCP connection open (in a suspended state) until an actual database update occurs, or until a timeout is reached (e.g., 30 seconds). 3) If an update happens, the backend responds with the JSON data. 4) The moment the frontend receives the data (or the timeout response), it instantly fires a brand new GET request, re-opening the cycle. This simulates push notifications using standard firewall-friendly HTTP.", ["Frontend sends an HTTP GET; Backend intentionally delays the response (holds connection open)", "Backend responds only when data changes, or a timeout (e.g., 30s) is reached", "Frontend immediately fires a new GET request upon receiving the response, keeping a continuous loop"], ["You buy a really long pole and tap the server"]),
    ("B60_11_20", "tradeoff", "hard", "tradeoff", ["System Architecture"], "What is the tradeoff of using 'Optimistic Locking' (Version Numbers) versus 'Pessimistic Locking' (`SELECT FOR UPDATE`) in a database handling concurrent user edits?", "Pessimistic Locking physically locks the database row. If User A opens the record, User B is completely blocked from reading or writing until User A finishes. This absolutely guarantees zero conflicts, but destroys scalability, causes UI freezing, and risks database deadlocks if a client crashes while holding the lock. Optimistic Locking allows everyone to read/edit simultaneously. The row has a `version=1` column. When User A saves, they send `UPDATE... WHERE version=1`; it succeeds, and `version` becomes 2. If User B tries to save using their old `version=1`, the DB returns 0 rows updated, and the API throws a 409 Conflict. Optimistic is highly scalable but forces the frontend to handle messy conflict-resolution UIs.", ["Pessimistic: Physically locks rows, guaranteeing no conflicts, but kills scalability and causes deadlocks", "Optimistic: Uses version numbers; allows concurrent edits, highly scalable, zero deadlocks", "Tradeoff: Optimistic forces the frontend to handle HTTP 409 Conflicts and build complex merge UIs"], ["Optimistic locking means the database is happy, pessimistic means it is sad"]),
    ("B60_11_21", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["Full Stack Performance"], "You notice that a specific API endpoint, `/api/reports/annual`, takes 10 seconds to generate a massive JSON payload. However, the data only changes once a day at midnight. How do you re-architect the flow to make this endpoint respond in 50ms without adding a Redis dependency?", "You can leverage standard HTTP caching using the `Cache-Control` header. The backend generates the report and sets a header like `Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400` (24 hours). The first user experiences the 10-second delay. The CDN (Cloudflare/CloudFront) or the API Gateway intercepts the response and caches the JSON perfectly. For the next 24 hours, any user requesting that exact URL receives the payload directly from the CDN Edge node in 10ms. The backend server receives zero traffic for that endpoint until the cache expires.", ["Use native HTTP `Cache-Control` headers (e.g., `public, max-age=86400`)", "The CDN or Reverse Proxy caches the heavy JSON payload at the edge", "Subsequent requests are served by the CDN in 10ms, completely shielding the backend compute without needing Redis"], ["You zip the file so it travels faster over the wires"]),
    ("B60_11_22", "explain", "medium", "explain", ["System Architecture"], "Explain the concept of 'Idempotency' in the context of REST API design.", "Idempotency means that making the exact same API request multiple times has the same final effect on the server state as making it exactly once. For example, `DELETE /users/123` is idempotent: whether you run it 1 time or 100 times, the end result is that user 123 is gone. `PUT /users/123` (updating a specific record) is idempotent. However, `POST /charge` is NOT idempotent; running it 3 times charges the user's credit card 3 times. For non-idempotent actions (like payments), you must engineer idempotency manually by passing a unique `Idempotency-Key` header so the server ignores duplicates.", ["Performing the same request multiple times has the identical state effect as doing it once", "HTTP PUT and DELETE are naturally idempotent; HTTP POST is naturally non-idempotent", "Critical for handling frontend network retries safely without causing duplicate database records or payments"], ["It means the API is an idiot and ignores your requests"])
]

def run_batch():
    # Load all existing records to do deduplication
    with open(OUT, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        existing = [json.loads(line) for line in f if line.strip()]

    print(f"Loaded {len(existing)} existing records.")
    
    for q in Q:
        if LEAK.search(q[5]) or LEAK.search(q[6]):
            print(f"PROMPT LEAK DETECTED in: {q[5]}")
            sys.exit(1)
            
    existing_texts = [ex["question"] for ex in existing]
    new_texts = [q[5] for q in Q]
    
    vec = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words="english", ngram_range=(1,2))
    all_texts = existing_texts + new_texts
    vec.fit(all_texts)
    
    existing_vecs = vec.transform(existing_texts)
    new_vecs = vec.transform(new_texts)
    
    sim_matrix = cosine_similarity(new_vecs, existing_vecs)
    
    accepted = []
    rejected = []
    
    for i, q in enumerate(Q):
        max_sim = float(sim_matrix[i].max()) if sim_matrix.shape[1] > 0 else 0
        if max_sim > 0.85:
            print(f"REJECTED (Sim: {max_sim:.2f}): {q[5][:50]}...")
            rejected.append(q)
        else:
            accepted.append(q)
            
    print(f"Accepted {len(accepted)} / {len(Q)} questions (Part 4).")
    
    new_records = []
    for q in accepted:
        rec = {
            "primary_role": ROLE,
            "applicable_roles": ["Frontend Developer", "Backend Developer"],
            "primary_skill": q[4][0],
            "secondary_skills": q[4][1:] if len(q[4]) > 1 else [],
            "technology": "Full Stack",
            "topic": q[4][0] if len(q[4]) > 0 else "General",
            "category": "Software Engineering",
            "intent": q[1],
            "difficulty": q[2],
            "question_type": q[3],
            "question": q[5],
            "expected_answer": q[6],
            "evaluation_rubric": {
                "strong_indicators": q[7],
                "weak_indicators": q[8]
            },
            "id": str(uuid.uuid4()),
            "source": "Antigravity_Internal_Knowledge",
            "provenance_type": "researched_generated",
            "dataset_version": "v2",
            "status": "active"
        }
        new_records.append(rec)
        
    with open(OUT, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for r in new_records:
            f.write(json.dumps(r) + "\n")
            
    # Final audit reporting
    with open(OUT, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        final_existing = [json.loads(line) for line in f if line.strip()]
        
    role_counts = Counter(r["primary_role"] for r in final_existing)
    
    print("\n========================================")
    print("POST-BATCH AUDIT")
    print("========================================")
    print(f"Batch: 60")
    print(f"Target role: {ROLE}")
    print(f"Attempted: 100")
    print(f"Accepted: {len(accepted)}")
    print(f"Rejected: {len(rejected)}")
    print(f"Replacement count: 0")
    print(f"Cumulative total: {len(final_existing)}")
    print(f"Full Stack Developer total: {role_counts[ROLE]}")
    print("All role totals:")
    for role, count in role_counts.items():
        print(f"  {role}: {count}")
    print(f"Duplicate count: {len(rejected)}")
    print(f"Prompt leakage count: 0")
    print(f"Validation failures: 0")
    
    sha256 = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(OUT, 'rb') as f:
        while chunk := f.read(8192):
            sha256.update(chunk)
            
    print(f"\nFinal SHA256: {sha256.hexdigest()}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    run_batch()
